<%#
  A TAB'S SLOT IN EDIT MODE (2026-09-09, tabs; spec §6). A separator in the flat list: it is a
  `.panel-slot`, so Sortable drags it by the same handle; it carries `data-kind="tab"` and
  `data-name`, which is all of its draft. No strip, no
  width, no eye, no pencil -- a tab has none of those (decision 5). Delete removes the separator
  only; its panels join the group above, and it asks first unless the tab is nameless and empty.
  The count hint is the controller's (countTabs).

  UP AND DOWN, LIKE A PANEL'S STRIP (2026-11-02, the UX audit's C10): a separator was the one slot
  only a drag could move, and the strip's own reason for Up/Down applies to it — ordering is never
  mouse-only. The same two actions, so `step` refocuses the button after the move exactly as it
  does on a panel.

  Locals: key, name.
%>
<div class="panel-slot panel-slot--tab panel--full" data-layout-editor-target="slot"
     data-key="<%= key %>" data-kind="tab" data-name="<%= name %>">
  <span class="panel-strip-handle" title="Drag to move" aria-hidden="true">⋮⋮</span>
  <span class="tab-slot-label">Tab</span>
  <input type="text" class="tab-name" maxlength="<%= Maker::ProjectKind::TAB_NAME_MAX %>" value="<%= name %>"
         placeholder="Name this tab" aria-label="Tab name" data-action="input->layout-editor#tabName">
  <span class="muted tab-slot-count" data-layout-editor-target="tabCount"></span>
  <button type="button" class="linkbtn small" data-action="layout-editor#up" aria-label="Move this tab up">▲</button>
  <button type="button" class="linkbtn small" data-action="layout-editor#down" aria-label="Move this tab down">▼</button>
  <button type="button" class="linkbtn small danger" data-action="layout-editor#deleteTab" aria-label="Delete this tab">Delete</button>
</div>
